  "course_key": "g3-sec1-math",
  "unit_key": "g3-sec1-n6",
  "position": 3,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Linear functions y = ax + b",
  "summary": "Interpret, evaluate and determine linear functions using their rate of change and constant term.",
  "outcomes": [
    "6.3"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "n6-lesson-02"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 50,
  "objectives": [
    "Interpret a and b in a linear function y = ax + b.",
    "Evaluate a function for a given input and solve for an input from an output.",
    "Determine a linear function from two input-output pairs."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "n6-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-functions-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Interpret the rate of change and starting value",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In y = ax + b, the coefficient a is the change in y for each increase of 1 in x, and b is the output when x = 0. The value b is therefore the y-intercept. Substitute input values using brackets, especially when x or a is negative."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "a=\\frac{\\text{change in }y}{\\text{change in }x},\\qquad b=y\\text{ when }x=0"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n6-lesson-03-function-parts"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A positive a produces a line that rises from left to right; a negative a produces a line that falls; a = 0 gives a horizontal line. Increasing |a| makes the line steeper when both axes use fixed scales."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-functions-evaluate-invert",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Evaluate an output or work backwards to an input",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To evaluate a function, replace x with the given input and follow the order of operations. To find an input from a known output, replace y and solve the resulting linear equation. Keep the equation balanced at every step."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "y=-3x+11,\\ x=-2\\Rightarrow y=-3(-2)+11=17"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A common error is to ignore the sign of the substituted value. Writing the substitution line before calculating makes the multiplication of two negative numbers visible."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-functions-from-values",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Determine a linear function from values",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Given two input-output pairs, first divide the change in output by the matching change in input to find a. Then substitute either complete pair into y = ax + b to find b. Never assume b from the first listed output unless that pair has x = 0."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "a=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1},\\qquad b=y_1-ax_1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Verify the finished rule using the other pair. If it fails, recheck the subtraction order and signs. Swapping both subtraction orders leaves the ratio unchanged, but swapping only one reverses the sign incorrectly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-functions-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: determine a function from two points",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A linear function has y = 7 when x = 2 and y = 19 when x = 5. Find its rule and then find the output when x = −2."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find the rate of change from the two pairs."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "a=\\frac{19-7}{5-2}=\\frac{12}{3}=4"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Use (2, 7) to find b."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "7=4(2)+b\\Rightarrow b=-1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Write the rule and substitute x = −2."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=4x-1,\\qquad y=4(-2)-1=-9"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The second original pair checks the rule because 4(5) − 1 = 19; the new output follows from the same constant rate."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-function-context-example",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: interpret a decreasing function",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A phone battery is 90% charged and loses 4 percentage points of charge per hour while a video plays. Let B be the charge, in %, after t hours. Write B as a function of t, find B after 6 hours, find when 30% remains, and state the domain."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The starting value is 90 and the rate is negative because the charge decreases."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "B=-4t+90"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute t = 6."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "B=-4(6)+90=66"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Set B = 30 and solve."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "30=-4t+90\\Rightarrow4t=60\\Rightarrow t=15\\text{ hours}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 4,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The battery is empty when B = 0, that is when t = 90 ÷ 4 = 22.5. The rule only makes sense for this time, so the domain is"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "0\\le t\\le22.5"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "After 15 hours the charge used is 4 × 15 = 60 percentage points, leaving 90 − 60 = 30%. Outside 0 ≤ t ≤ 22.5 the rule would give a negative charge or describe a time before the video started."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-functions-recall",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For y = -2x + 7, find y when x = 4."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "-1",
        "canonical_latex": "-1",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Substitute x = 4: y = -2(4) + 7 = -1."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-lesson-03-summary",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Read a as the change in output per unit input and b as the output at x = 0. Substitute with brackets, and solve a linear equation when the output is given instead."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "From two pairs, find a using matched changes, then find b from one whole pair. Verify the final function with the unused pair and interpret the sign and domain in context."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
